From 60d8c7713727fb6224b84d158d32bd1d206d9c24 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 14:23:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 233/274] V2 runner sampling: Triton 3.7's block layout, by the
 load's alignment

The image runs Triton 3.7.1, whose coalescing pass lays a block out by the
logits load's specialization: when the base and the row stride are
divisible by 16, a thread holds 16-byte runs (four FP32 or eight 16-bit
values, runs 512 or 1024 apart), else one element 128 apart. The argmax
combines a thread's elements in that order, which decides the index a
block with NaN reports, so the Gumbel sampler and the sink candidates take
the run from the same rule and load each whole run as one vector, as
Triton does; so does the temperature kernel. The candidates and policy
kernels give every input its own length (the drafts may be longer than the
rows), and greedy verification takes int32 or int64 drafts.

The tests compare both strides at every vocabulary, partial runs at the
tail included.

